    "summary": "11, 2001, attacks, including documents showing officials were weighing potential liability from toxic exposure while trying to reassure the public about the safety of Lower Manhattan. One memo prepared in October 2001 for Robert Harding, a deputy mayor under Rudy Guiliani, estimated that the city could face 35,000 potential plaintiffs and contemplated seeking federal protection from “toxic tort cases that might arise in the next few decades.” Another city document from May 2002 said that officials would avoid using the word “risk” in correspondence about a cleanup program because doing so “would defeat the purpose of creating public confidence.” An estimated 400,000 people, including first responders, were exposed to toxic contaminants and other hazards in the days, weeks and months following the attacks, according to the CDC. Fires in the towers’ debris piles burned for months after the attacks, releasing carcinogenic byproducts.",
